<TopicPage slug="subjects/operations-research/foundational/linear-programming">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the first lesson in Operations Research, and it assumes nothing beyond school
    algebra — lines, slopes and solving two equations in two unknowns. Everything else is built
    here, with one running example carried from first formulation to its economic meaning.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>formulate a linear program: choose decision variables, write a linear objective, and translate limited resources into linear inequalities;</li>
    <li>state the four modelling assumptions — proportionality, additivity, divisibility, certainty — and recognise when a real decision violates them;</li>
    <li>draw the geometry: constraints as half-spaces, the feasible polygon, and iso-profit lines sliding to the optimal vertex;</li>
    <li>explain the corner-point theorem and why it licenses the simplex method's vertex-to-vertex walk;</li>
    <li>interpret duality: shadow prices, complementary slackness, and the questions a price list answers that a plan cannot.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit: the algebra is the easy part. At every step we ask what the symbols mean in the
    world — the professional skill is making the algebra true of the world.
  </p>

  <h2>The workhorse of modern decision-making</h2>
  <p>
    An unreasonable share of the world's logistics, manufacturing, scheduling, blending and crew
    planning runs on a single mathematical idea: <strong>linear programming</strong> (LP) —
    maximise or minimise a linear objective (profit, cost, distance) subject to linear
    constraints. Why linear? Partly because so many real trade-offs are proportional (twice the
    output takes twice the labour), but mostly because linear models <em>solve</em>: not by
    guesswork, but by a geometric theory that guarantees the optimum can be found, and an
    algorithm — the simplex method, George Dantzig, 1947 — that turns "solve" into a walk
    between corners of a shape. Problems with thousands of variables and constraints are solved
    to provable optimality in seconds on a laptop, which is why LP became the first tool
    operations research teaches rather than one among many.
  </p>
  <p>
    The intellectual arc of the field fits in one sentence: <em>formulation</em> turns a messy
    decision into algebra; <em>geometry</em> shows the algebra is a shape whose corners hold the
    answers; <em>the simplex method</em> walks the corners efficiently; and <em>duality</em>
    reveals that every optimisation secretly contains a second problem — a price list — whose
    solution says what each bottleneck is worth. This page builds that arc with one running
    example — a furniture workshop choosing how many tables and chairs to make — then looks at
    where LP ends and its harder relatives begin.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="variables, objective, constraints, vertex, duality" widget="equationStep" config={lpCfg} />

  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the five cards are the arc of the whole page in miniature —
    and the order is not negotiable. Variables come first because every later line is built from
    them; the vertex claim in step 4 is a theorem rather than an observation; and step 5 is not
    extra mathematics but the economic payoff. Keep the cards in view as a map of the sections
    below.
  </p>

  <h2>Formulation: turning a decision into algebra</h2>
  <p>
    Every LP is assembled from the same four parts. <strong>Decision variables</strong>: the
    quantities you may choose — x<sub>1</sub> tables and x<sub>2</sub> chairs per week in our
    running example. <strong>An objective function</strong>: a linear expression to maximise or
    minimise — say profit of 30 per table and 20 per chair, so maximise
    30x<sub>1</sub> + 20x<sub>2</sub>. <strong>Constraints</strong>: linear inequalities
    capturing limited resources — carpentry allows 40 hours weekly (2 hours per table, 1 per
    chair: 2x<sub>1</sub> + x<sub>2</sub> ≤ 40), finishing allows 30 hours (x<sub>1</sub> +
    x<sub>2</sub> ≤ 30) — plus <strong>non-negativity</strong> (x<sub>1</sub>, x<sub>2</sub> ≥ 0;
    you cannot make negative tables). The translation is the analyst's real craft, because it is
    where modelling errors enter: variables must be defined with units and time frames (tables
    <em>per week</em>), constraints must capture genuinely binding limits, and everything the
    objective or constraints touch must behave <em>proportionally</em>.
  </p>
  <p>
    That last requirement — proportionality — is what makes a model linear, and LP courses spend
    real time on its boundary. The standard assumptions: <strong>proportionality</strong>
    (contributions scale with the variable, no setup costs or quantity discounts),
    <strong>additivity</strong> (effects of variables simply sum, no interaction terms),
    <strong>divisibility</strong> (fractional answers make sense — true for blending tonnes of
    ore, false for "build one more aircraft"), and <strong>certainty</strong> (the coefficients
    are known numbers, not gambles — relaxed by stochastic programming). Formulation is also an
    act of honest scoping: the modeller decides what the variables are allowed to mean, and a
    well-posed LP is often worth as much as its solution because it makes the trade-offs
    <em>explicit</em> — the meetings it disciplines are part of its value.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "the solver returned 10.5 tables — it must be broken."
  Fractional output feels like an error, but it is the divisibility assumption doing exactly what
  it was told: LP solutions are only required to make sense as <em>continuous</em> quantities.
  For ore blending or fuel mixing, 10.5 tonnes is a fine answer; for aircraft, it is nonsense —
  and the fix is a different model (integer programming), not suspicion of the arithmetic. Knowing
  which world your variables live in is part of formulation, decided before any solver runs.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> a decision has become four lines of algebra — variables, objective,
    constraints, non-negativity. Next, the geometry that makes those four lines solvable.
  </p>

  <h2>Geometry: constraints cut the plane, corners win</h2>
  <p>
    In two variables the whole theory is visible, which is why it is taught drawn on paper. Each
    constraint is a line that cuts the plane in half (a <strong>half-space</strong>); stacking
    all the halves leaves the <strong>feasible region</strong> — a polygon (in higher dimensions,
    a polyhedron) containing exactly the choices that obey every constraint. The objective is a
    family of parallel lines — every point on one line earns the same profit, so the lines are
    <em>iso-profit</em> lines. Maximising means sliding the iso-profit line outward as far as it
    can go while still touching the feasible region, and where it last touches is a theorem, not
    a coincidence: <strong>the optimum of a linear program lies at a vertex</strong> (extreme
    point) of the feasible region. If the objective were parallel to a constraint, a whole edge
    ties for best — and a vertex of that edge still wins. This <em>corner-point theorem</em> is
    the licence for everything that follows: instead of searching infinitely many points, search
    finitely many corners.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="constraints, feasible region and the optimal vertex" widget="plotter" config={feasibleCfg} />

  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> two things and then a habit. First, each constraint line
    divides the plane: keeping the side that contains the origin (the obeyable side) shows the
    region under <em>both</em> lines is the feasible set. In the plotted window the steeper
    material line lies below the labour line everywhere — so material is the constraint that
    actually binds here, while labour has slack. Binding versus non-binding is visible geometry
    before it is algebra. Second, the teal iso-profit line is one member of a parallel family;
    the optimum sits where the last member of the family that still touches the feasible region
    touches it — always at a corner. The habit: use the hover crosshair to read the corners and
    compute the profit at each yourself — with two variables you can out-perform the solver by
    hand, which is the point of drawing the picture.
  </p>
  <p>
    Finishing the running example by hand makes the theorem concrete. The feasible region's
    vertices are the origin; (20, 0), where carpentry alone binds; (0, 30), where finishing alone
    binds; and the intersection of both constraints — solving 2x<sub>1</sub> + x<sub>2</sub> = 40
    with x<sub>1</sub> + x<sub>2</sub> = 30 gives x<sub>1</sub> = 10, x<sub>2</sub> = 20.
    Evaluating profit at each: 0; 600; 600; and 30(10) + 20(20) = <strong>700 at (10, 20)</strong>
    — ten tables and twenty chairs, both resources fully used.
  </p>
  <p>
    With two variables, evaluating four corners is trivial; with two thousand variables and as
    many constraints, the vertex count is combinatorial, and evaluating them all is hopeless. The
    <strong>simplex method</strong> is the escape: start at any vertex, look along the edges
    leaving it, move along whichever edge improves the objective fastest, and repeat. At each
    step the profit rises; when no leaving edge improves it, the current vertex is optimal — the
    corner-point theorem guarantees that stopping rule is correct. Algebraically, the method adds
    <strong>slack variables</strong> (measuring unused capacity of each resource) so the
    inequalities become equations, then "pivots" one variable into the basis and another out —
    bookkeeping that a computer performs at industrial scale. Two honest caveats belong in a
    first course: simplex is spectacular in practice yet has exponential worst cases (the
    Klee–Minty cube), and the <strong>interior-point methods</strong> introduced by Karmarkar in
    1984 — walking <em>through</em> the polytope rather than around its edges — solved provably
    in polynomial time and now share the heavy lifting with simplex in commercial solvers.
    Neither caveat changes the geometry; both explain why the 1980s doubled the size of solvable
    problems overnight.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "the simplex method must check every point in the feasible
  region — that is what solving means." Optimisation does sound like exhaustive search. But the
  corner-point theorem deletes the interior entirely (an optimum is always at a vertex), and the
  improving-walk rule deletes almost all the vertices: the method visits a handful, each with
  profit strictly better than the last, and stops the moment no edge improves. What looks like a
  brute-force problem is a guided walk — the difference between checking a million corners and
  visiting a few dozen.</p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that LP is "linear algebra with inequalities" — a mechanical
  exercise. Formulation is modelling judgement (choosing variables, spotting binding resources,
  refusing linearity where it does not hold), and duality is economic interpretation, not algebra
  hygiene. The mathematics is the easy part; the professional skill is making the algebra true of
  the world.</p>

  <h2>Duality and sensitivity: every plan has a price list</h2>
  <p>
    LP's deepest idea is that every maximisation problem (the <strong>primal</strong>) is
    shadowed by a minimisation problem (the <strong>dual</strong>) built from the same data: one
    dual variable per constraint, interpreting naturally as a <em>price</em>. For the furniture
    example the dual asks: what should carpentry hours and finishing hours be worth, per hour, so
    that no product is more profitable to make than its inputs justify, while minimising the
    total imputed value of the capacity? Solving both yields <strong>strong duality</strong>:
    the optimal objective values coincide — the best plan and the best price list meet exactly.
    The dual variables at the optimum are the <strong>shadow prices</strong>: for our example
    both come out at 10 — one more carpentry hour (within range) adds 10 to weekly profit, as
    does one more finishing hour. Every binding constraint gets a positive shadow price;
    non-binding ones get zero (unused capacity is worth nothing — the free lunch the model
    refuses to serve).
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "more of any resource must add value — buy everything
  cheap and expand." The shadow-price logic says otherwise: value flows only through constraints
  that actually bind. If the workshop's demand caps chairs at 15 and the plan already leaves
  finishing hours idle, an extra finishing hour is worth exactly zero — buying it raises cost
  and nothing else. Always ask which constraints bind before asking what more capacity is worth;
  the algebraic statement (a resource with slack has zero price) is called complementary
  slackness, and it is the guard-rail on every capacity negotiation.</p>
  <p>
    This turns a solution into a negotiation tool, and sensitivity analysis extends it. The
    <strong>shadow price</strong> says what a resource is worth at the margin: bidding for
    overtime below 10 per hour raises profit; above it, loses money. Airlines run the same logic
    on gates and landing slots; refineries on crude qualities; streaming logistics on warehouse
    capacity. <strong>Reduced costs</strong> price the variables not in the plan — a third
    product whose profit would need to rise by 4 per unit before it earns a place in the mix. And
    <strong>ranging</strong> asks how far a coefficient can move before the current basis stops
    being optimal, defining the validity limits of every shadow price. Complementary slackness
    ties it all in a sentence: a resource with slack has zero price, and a product with positive
    price must be produced — the algebraic echo of market clearing, which is why duality,
    discovered by von Neumann in 1947 (and independently embedded in Kantorovich's Soviet
    planning work, recognised by the 1975 Nobel prize), is taught in economics departments as
    often as in engineering ones.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Why must a formulation decide units and time frames for its variables before anything else?</li>
    <li>A blending model returns "ship 1000.5 tonnes". A delivery model returns "send 1000.5 trucks". Which answer is suspect, and why?</li>
    <li>In the furniture example, which resources bind at the optimum (10, 20), and what are their shadow prices?</li>
    <li>Why does the simplex method never need to examine interior points of the feasible region?</li>
    <li>A constraint has slack at the optimum. What is its shadow price, and what does that mean economically?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Every objective coefficient and constraint is measured
    <em>per unit of the variable</em>; without units and a time frame the rates are undefined and
    the model silently mixes incompatible quantities. (2) The trucks: trucks are indivisible, so
    a fractional answer violates divisibility and calls for integer programming; tonnes of
    blending are continuous, so 1000.5 is fine. (3) Both — carpentry 2(10) + 20 = 40 and
    finishing 10 + 20 = 30 are exactly exhausted; both shadow prices are 10 per hour. (4) The
    corner-point theorem: a linear objective attains its optimum at a vertex, so interior points
    can never beat the best corner. (5) Zero — slack means the resource is not limiting, so extra
    units of it add nothing to the objective; spend instead on the binding constraints.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Linear programming maximises or minimises a linear objective under linear constraints — the solvable workhorse of operations research.</li>
    <li>Formulation has four parts (variables, objective, constraints, non-negativity) and four assumptions (proportionality, additivity, divisibility, certainty); formulation is where the modelling judgement lives.</li>
    <li>Geometrically: constraints are half-spaces, the feasible set is a polygon, and the objective is a family of parallel iso-profit lines.</li>
    <li>Corner-point theorem: the optimum lies at a vertex — finite search replaces infinite search.</li>
    <li>The simplex method walks vertex to vertex with profit rising at every step; slack variables turn inequalities into equations; Karmarkar's interior-point methods (1984) share the load today.</li>
    <li>Duality prices the constraints: shadow prices, strong duality, complementary slackness — a plan plus a price list, each proving the other.</li>
    <li>Sensitivity analysis (reduced costs, ranging) says what a solution is worth and how far it can be trusted.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The next lesson, on network flows, meets LP's most successful special cases — shortest path,
    maximum flow, minimum-cost flow — where structure lets specialised algorithms outrun general
    solvers. After that, integer optimisation handles the "build or not" world where
    divisibility fails, using the LP of this page as its engine at every step. Nonlinear
    optimisation later relaxes linearity itself — and convexity, LP's quiet superpower, is the
    idea that survives the relaxation.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    A second worked example shows why structure matters as much as size. The
    <strong>transportation problem</strong> — ship from m factories to n warehouses at the least
    total cost, with each factory's supply and each warehouse's demand fixed — is an LP, but its
    constraint matrix is so sparse and regular (each variable appears in exactly two constraints:
    its origin and its destination) that specialised structure takes over. The matrix is
    <em>totally unimodular</em>, which guarantees that the LP's optimal vertex is automatically
    integral: no fractional shipments appear, no integer programming is needed, and the simplex
    method specialises into the network simplex, which walks the spanning trees of the underlying
    graph and solves problems with millions of arcs. Assignment, shortest path and maximum flow
    inherit the same property — maximum flow in a network is literally an LP whose integer
    optimum is guaranteed, though in practice special-purpose algorithms such as
    Ford–Fulkerson beat the general machinery on their own turf. The general lesson, and the
    reason OR courses teach transportation separately, is that recognising hidden structure
    routinely outperforms raw solver power — a special case that took minutes as a general LP
    becomes milliseconds as a network.
  </p>
  <p>
    The LP rabbit hole goes far. <strong>LP as a subroutine</strong>: integer programming — the
    "build or not" world of facilities, capital budgeting and scheduling — is NP-hard, but its
    standard solution machinery (branch and bound, branch and cut) runs on the <em>LP
    relaxation</em> at every node, so LP quality directly determines integer solve times;
    decomposition methods (Dantzig–Wolfe, Benders) split enormous LPs into manageable pieces by
    exploiting the dual. <strong>Convexity's promise</strong>: LP is the sharpest special case of
    convex optimisation — local optimality implies global, duality is clean, and the same
    geometry (half-spaces, polytopes) generalises to quadratic and conic programs used in
    portfolio optimisation and control. And the frontier is practical rather than theoretical:
    modern solvers (CPLEX, Gurobi, and strong open-source cousins) preprocess, presolve and cut
    aggressively, so a first-course model that took 1960s computers minutes now takes
    microseconds — which is why the durable skills of this unit are formulation discipline,
    geometric intuition, and the habit of asking not just "what is the optimal plan?" but "what
    is the plan's price list, and within what ranges can I trust it?" Those three questions,
    asked in that order, are linear programming.
  </p>

